"A chord is three or more notes played together.": "796b312bfea3",
"A chord is two or more notes sounding at the same time. A triad has three notes stacked in thirds: the root, then a third above, then a fifth above. A major triad is a major third and a perfect fifth above the root, so from C it is C, E, G. In just intonation its ratio is 4 : 5 : 6. A minor triad has a minor third instead, so from C it is C, E♭, G. Only the middle note is different, by one half step. Major and minor triads are the most common triads in Western music.": "69494bb27417",
"A dissonant interval sounds tense and wants to move.": "3562a5ff77e9",
"A perfect fifth has a ratio of 3 to 2.": "bcf5f5cee046",
"A triad has three notes: a root, a third, and a fifth.": "51b1c2e86f78",
"An interval is the difference in pitch between two sounds. You can also say it as a frequency ratio. Two notes an octave apart have a ratio of 2 to 1. A perfect fifth is 3 to 2, a perfect fourth is 4 to 3, a major third is 5 to 4, and a minor third is 6 to 5. You met 2 to 1 and 3 to 2 on the string in lesson 1.": "6861c3e3a65c",
"An interval is the difference in pitch between two sounds. You can also say it as a frequency ratio. Two notes an octave apart have a ratio of 2 to 1. A perfect fifth is 3 to 2, a perfect fourth is 4 to 3, a major third is 5 to 4, and a minor third is 6 to 5. You met 2 to 1 and 3 to 2 on the string in lesson 1. An interval’s name counts the notes it covers, with both ends included. From C to E is a third, because C, D, and E are three notes. In semitones, a minor third is 3, a major third is 4, a perfect fourth is 5, and a perfect fifth is 7. A perfect fifth is at the start of “Twinkle, Twinkle, Little Star.” A piano is not exactly in these ratios: its fifth is 700 cents, but 3 to 2 is about 701.96 cents, and its major third is about 14 cents wider than 5 to 4. Musicians call a stable combination of notes a consonance, a point of rest. They call a tense combination a dissonance, which seems to want to move on. Consonance is often associated with simple ratios. Look at the lab: when two notes are an octave apart, the combined wave repeats as fast as the lower note. For a fifth it lines up again every 1/110 of a second. For a half step the pattern takes much longer, and the two notes seem to rub against each other. When two notes are almost the same, you hear something else. The sound gets louder and softer, again and again, and this is called beats. The beat rate is the difference between the two frequencies: 220 Hz and 224 Hz make 4 beats per second. Slow beats are heard as the loudness changing, and faster ones, above about 20 per second, as a rough, fluttering sound. Piano tuners listen for beats to know when two strings are in tune. These beats are not the beat of a song, which you learned about in lesson 3. A chord is two or more notes sounding at the same time. A triad has three notes stacked in thirds: the root, then a third above, then a fifth above. A major triad is a major third and a perfect fifth above the root, so from C it is C, E, G. In just intonation its ratio is 4 : 5 : 6. A minor triad has a minor third instead, so from C it is C, E♭, G. Only the middle note is different, by one half step. Major and minor triads are the most common triads in Western music. Is the pleasure of consonance built in, or learned? Researchers still debate it. In a 2016 study, people of the Tsimane’, an Amazonian society with little exposure to Western music, rated consonant and dissonant chords as equally pleasant. But they still disliked rough sounds. Dissonance is not simply bad, either. Dissonances often play a decisive role in making music pleasant. So listen with open ears, and let your own listening tell you what you find beautiful.": "298bec8d7158",
